<h1><strong>Inoculating against fatalistic beliefs: a comparative test of n=
arrative and non-narrative strategies</strong></h1>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-1" data-idx=3D"1">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">1</span>
      <p><strong>Minyi Chen* , and Hye Kyung Kim</strong></p>
<p>Wee Kim Wee School of Communication and Information, Nanyang Technologic=
al University, Singapore</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-2" data-idx=3D"2">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">2</span>
      <p>*Corresponding author: Wee Kim Wee School of Communication and Inf=
ormation, Nanyang Technological University, 31 Nanyang Link, 637718, Singap=
ore. Email: <a href=3D"mailto:minyi001@e.ntu.edu.sg">minyi001@e.ntu.edu.sg<=
/a>.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-3" data-idx=3D"3">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">3</span>
      <h3><strong>Abstract</strong></h3>
<p>**This study compares the effectiveness of narrative and non-narrative i=
noculation messages in reducing fatalistic beliefs and fostering resistance=
 to conflicting cancer information. A two-phase randomized experiment (**<e=
m>N</em> <strong>=3D 572 in Phase 1;</strong>  <em>n</em> <strong>=3D 332 i=
n Phase 2) tested their effects on counterarguing against attack, cancer fa=
talism, perceived efficacy, and intentions to adopt cancer risk-reduction b=
ehaviors. Compared with control, both inoculation messages reduced cancer f=
atalism, enhanced perceived efficacy, and increased certain intentions to a=
dopt certain risk-reduction behaviors. Narrative inoculation elicited more =
counterarguing against attack than the non-narrative counterpart. Inoculati=
on effects also varied by baseline fatalism, functioning therapeutically by=
 immediately reducing initially high fatalism and, more modestly, prophylac=
tically by maintaining initially low fatalism. These findings extend inocul=
ation theory by elucidating the role of message format in sustaining resist=
ance to negative media effects, and by demonstrating the theory's potential=
 dual prophylactic=E2=80=93therapeutic functionality in a fatalistic belief=
 context.</strong></p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-4" data-idx=3D"4">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">4</span>
      <p>The contemporary media environment exposes audiences to a vast and=
 often conflicting body of health information. Claims about disease prevent=
ion, detection, and treatment frequently contradict one another across news=
 and digital platforms, sowing uncertainty and confusion (Han et al., 2007)=
. Such discord can erode trust in expert guidance and confidence in prevent=
ive action (Iles et al., 2022). In cancer communication, for example, incon=
sistent messages about lifestyle and cancer risks have been linked to fatal=
ism=E2=80=94the pessimistic belief that cancer occurrence is predetermined =
and survival lies beyond personal control, a belief that in turn discourage=
s preventive action (Iles  et al., 2022; Niederdeppe et al., 2010). Fatalis=
m thus offers a concrete context for studying resistance to conflicting inf=
ormation, yet the theoretical challenge runs deeper: how can communication =
strategies proactively equip individuals to withstand the demotivating effe=
cts of inconsistent media messages?</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-5" data-idx=3D"5">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">5</span>
      <p>Inoculation theory offers one promising avenue. Since McGuire's (1=
961, 1964) seminal work, inoculation has served as a well-documented framew=
ork for building resistance to persuasion, forewarning audiences of an impe=
nding challenge and supplying weakened counterarguments paired with refutat=
ions. To date, however, inoculation research has largely relied on exposito=
ry messages to defend specific issue attitudes against direct attack, leavi=
ng two questions underexplored. First, can inoculation extend beyond a spec=
ific attitude to confer resistance against broader belief orientations that=
 media environments cultivate indirectly? Second, does message format matte=
r=E2=80=94can narratives strengthen or weaken inoculation's core mechanisms=
 of threat and counterarguing?</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-6" data-idx=3D"6">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">6</span>
      <p>The second question is the more vexed, because the very qualities =
that make narratives persuasive sit in tension with how inoculation works. =
Narratives, a mainstay of communication practice, exert influence through t=
ransportation into story worlds and identification with characters (Cohen, =
2001; Green &amp;  Brock, 2000); they characteristically overcome audiences=
' resistance to persuasion by reducing counterarguing (Moyer-Gus=C3=A9,  20=
08). Yet these qualities complicate their role in inoculation, which depend=
s on stimulating active counterarguing to prepare audiences for later persu=
asive attacks (Compton &amp; Ivanov,  2013). Faced with this tension, some =
scholars suggest that narratives may bolster inoculation by deepening engag=
ement with refutational content (Compton &amp; Mason, 2020), whereas others=
 caution that immersion may blunt the cognitive counterarguing on which ino=
culation's durable effects depend (Moyer-Gus=C3=A9 &amp;  Nabi, 2010). Desp=
ite these debates, direct empirical comparisons of narrative and non-narrat=
ive inoculation messages remain scarce.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-7" data-idx=3D"7">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">7</span>
      <p>The present study addresses these gaps by testing inoculation agai=
nst the fatalistic beliefs that conflicting cancer information can induce, =
with the aim of advancing communication scholarship along three lines. Firs=
t, it examines inoculation's capacity to protect against a broader health b=
elief orientation shaped by conflicting media content, extending the scope =
of inoculation theory. Second, it provides a direct comparison of narrative=
 and <span id=3D"page-1-0"></span>non-narrative inoculation messages, testi=
ng whether narrative mechanisms enhance or undermine inoculation processes.=
 Third, it investigates whether inoculation functions prophylactically and =
therapeutically across individuals who differ in baseline belief, clarifyin=
g its boundary conditions and practical value. Together, these lines of inq=
uiry aim to deepen understanding of how communication can build resistance =
in an environment characterized by information abundance and contradiction.=
</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-8" data-idx=3D"8">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">8</span>
      <h1><strong>Building resistance against conflicting cancer informatio=
n and fatalistic beliefs</strong></h1>
<p>The proliferation of health information has made encounters with contrad=
ictory messages increasingly common. Cancer communication is a telling case=
, in which screening guidelines, lifestyle risk factors, and treatment prot=
ocols are frequently shifting and revised. In a large national sample, more=
 than half of respondents perceived cancer-prevention advice as inconsisten=
t (Gollust et al., 2023). Lay audiences often struggle to reconcile such in=
consistencies, which can erode trust in health authorities, reduce adherenc=
e to preventive guidelines, and breed doubt about whether prevention is wor=
thwhile (e.g., Nagler et al., 2019).</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-9" data-idx=3D"9">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">9</span>
      <p>Exposure to such conflict has been further linked to fatalism, a v=
iew that cancer is predetermined and beyond personal control. Long recogniz=
ed as a barrier to prevention (Chen &amp; Kim, 2024), fatalism can itself b=
e cultivated by media exposure (Chen &amp; Kim, 2024; Niederdeppe et al., 2=
010), particularly when audiences encounter contradictory cancer stories (e=
.g., Iles et al., 2022). Experimental evidence bears this out: conflicting =
information heightens fatalism, most sharply when the contradictions origin=
ate in evidence or from a single source, prompting people to doubt whether =
experts know how to prevent cancer at all (Iles et al., 2022). Such finding=
s highlight that demotivating beliefs can arise from the structure of the i=
nformation environment, not only from cultural predisposition.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-10" data-idx=3D"10">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">10</span>
      <p>Existing evidence points to a plausible mechanism underlying this =
effect. Conflicting information heightens perceived ambiguity, a state most=
 people find aversive (Han et al., 2007). Ambiguity raises cognitive load a=
s audiences strain to reconcile incompatible claims, producing frustration,=
 decision paralysis, or disengagement (Barnwell et al., 2022). When prevent=
ion advice appears unstable, this disengagement can harden into fatalism, a=
 defensive avoidance of the discomfort that unresolved uncertainty imposes.=
 Through this route, conflicting information can undermine both efficacy be=
liefs and preventive motivation.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-11" data-idx=3D"11">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">11</span>
      <p>This study employs cancer fatalism as a focal context for addressi=
ng broader theoretical questions about resistance to adverse media effects.=
 If conflicting information can cultivate fatalism, then effective communic=
ation strategies must build resilience proactively, fortifying audiences be=
fore such beliefs take hold. Inoculation theory provides a promising framew=
ork for this task, offering a way to forewarn individuals of impending chal=
lenges and equip them with refutations that preserve their sense of efficac=
y.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-12" data-idx=3D"12">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">12</span>
      <h1><strong>Inoculation theory</strong></h1>
<p>Inoculation theory provides a strategic framework for building resistanc=
e to persuasion by preemptively exposing individuals to weakened forms of f=
orthcoming threats, together with refutations (McGuire, 1961, 1964). Rooted=
 in a medical analogy, inoculation functions like a vaccine: by confronting=
 people with a manageable threat to their beliefs, it stimulates defenses t=
hat render them resilient to stronger challenges in the future. Over six de=
cades of research have established it as one of the most robust and versati=
le theories of resistance in communication, with applications spanning poli=
tics, advertising, and health (Compton, 2025), including efforts to counter=
 misinformation (van der Linden et al., 2017).</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-minor" id=3D"para-13" data-idx=3D"13"=
>
      <span class=3D"para-idx">13</span>
      <p>Classic inoculation messages include two elements: forewarning and=
 refutational preemption. Forewarning alerts recipients to a threat that th=
eir position may be challenged, whereas refutational preemption supplies we=
akened counterarguments and rebuttals in advance (McGuire, 1961, 1964). Rec=
ent theorizing clarifies that threat is inoculation's core mechanism: the r=
ecognition that one's position is vulnerable to challenge motivates resista=
nce (Compton, 2025). Forewarning is one means of generating this threat tha=
t motivates resistance, while refutational preemption provides recipients w=
ith material that can stimulate counterargument to resist later attacks. Cr=
ucially, inoculation's protection is not confined to the specific counterar=
guments contained in the message; because it extends an umbrella of protect=
ion to novel attacks as well (Compton, 2025), its effects rest on a broader=
 and more active resistance process than the mere recall of prepackaged reb=
uttals. Counterarguing is therefore an important response that threat activ=
ates within this broader resistance dynamic.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-14" data-idx=3D"14">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">14</span>
      <p>This clarification bears directly on the present study, where the =
anticipated challenge is not a single counterargument but the conflicting c=
ancer information and fatalistic interpretations that undermine confidence =
in prevention. Building on threat as the core mechanism keeps the study gro=
unded in the classic framework while allowing it to engage the broader info=
rmational and belief-related challenges of contemporary communication. Late=
r work has refined how threat is operationalized, distinguishing apprehensi=
ve responses from the more motivational forms that better capture its role =
as a catalyst for resistance (e.g., Banas &amp; Richards, 2017). Taken toge=
ther, these developments suggest that inoculation equips individuals with b=
oth the motivation and the cognitive resources to withstand challenges to t=
heir views, in contexts where challenges may arise not only as direct count=
erarguments but also as uncertainty, inconsistency, and shifting interpreta=
tions.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-15" data-idx=3D"15">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">15</span>
      <p>Recent scholarship underscores inoculation's relevance to these co=
nditions. Gretton et al. (2021) found that forewarning messages can mitigat=
e the negative effects of changing COVID-19 guidance on trust and complianc=
e intentions, and Nagler et al. (2023) showed that explaining the scientifi=
c process in advance helps audiences interpret conflicting information more=
 constructively. Inoculation messages that preemptively expose politicizing=
 tactics likewise reduce confusion and negative emotions in contested domai=
ns such as vaccination, pandemics, and climate change (e.g., van der Linden=
 et al., 2017). Collectively, <span id=3D"page-2-0"></span>this work highli=
ghts inoculation's adaptability across contexts marked by inconsistency.</p=
>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-16" data-idx=3D"16">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">16</span>
      <p>Important theoretical questions nonetheless remain. Most inoculati=
on studies have targeted discrete attitudes and specific opinions about pol=
icies, products, or behaviors, leaving open whether inoculation's protectiv=
e effects extend to the broader belief orientations that cumulative media e=
xposure cultivates. Emerging research points to the potential utility of in=
oculation beyond discrete attitudes. For example, inoculation was found to =
reduce generic conspiracist beliefs (Mason et al., 2024), protect against u=
ndesired psychological states such as public speaking anxiety (Jackson et a=
l., 2017), and address moral orientations such as resisting moral disengage=
ment and promoting ethical behavior (Jones et al., 2026).</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-17" data-idx=3D"=
17">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">17</span>
      <p>Even so, inoculation's application to health-related interpretive =
schemas remains limited. Cancer fatalism is one such schema: it reflects a =
durable orientation that shapes how individuals interpret uncertainty about=
 cancer prevention. Extending inoculation here tests whether threat and cou=
nterarguing can fortify resistance not to a discrete persuasive attack but =
to an orientation built up through repeated exposure to conflicting health =
information. If effective, the approach would demonstrate inoculation's cap=
acity to protect belief structures that govern long-term engagement with he=
alth messages.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-18" data-idx=3D"=
18">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">18</span>
      <p>Moreover, inoculation is especially well suited to countering fata=
listic responses to conflicting information. Fatalism is linked to low effi=
cacy and functions as a form of defensive avoidance when individuals feel i=
ncapable of effective action (Witte et al., 2001). Inoculation addresses th=
is directly: its explicit refutations of fatalistic counterarguments implic=
itly bolster efficacy, affirming that prevention remains possible and worth=
while. By pairing a manageable threat (the warning that conflicting informa=
tion may pull one toward fatalism) with a strong efficacy appeal grounded i=
n evidence-based refutation, inoculation encourages adaptive information pr=
ocessing in place of avoidance, positioning it to forestall the demotivatin=
g effects of conflicting cancer information.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-19" data-idx=3D"19">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">19</span>
      <p>Despite this fit, inoculation has rarely been applied to fatalisti=
c beliefs. By testing whether its mechanisms hold in this domain, the prese=
nt study extends the theory to one of the defining challenges of communicat=
ion in an age of information abundance and contradiction. The following hyp=
othesis is thus proposed:</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-20" data-idx=3D"20">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">20</span>
      <p>H1: Compared to the control group, inoculated participants will sh=
ow: (a) greater counterarguing against attack, (b) reduced cancer fatalism,=
 (c) higher perceived efficacy in cancer prevention, and (d) greater intent=
ions to adopt cancer risk-reduction behaviors.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-minor" id=3D"para-21" data-idx=3D"21"=
>
      <span class=3D"para-idx">21</span>
      <h1><strong>Narrative inoculation messages</strong></h1>
<p>Narratives have long been recognized as powerful tools of persuasion. As=
 connected sequences of events featuring characters and outcomes, narrative=
s influence audiences not through explicit claims but by drawing them into =
story worlds and characters' perspectives. Research on narrative persuasion=
 shows that such immersion can shape beliefs by reducing resistance and mod=
eling behaviors in ways expository messages do not (Green  &amp; Brock, 200=
0; Moyer-Gus=C3=A9, 2008). These qualities have made narratives prominent i=
n communication campaigns, advocacy, and entertainment-education. Two mecha=
nisms are central to narrative influence. Transportation is the state of ab=
sorption in a story world, in which attention and emotion shift toward its =
unfolding events (Green &amp; Brock, 2000); identification is the adoption =
of a character's perspective, such that the audience experiences the story =
through that character's thoughts, feelings, and choices (Cohen, 2001). Tog=
ether, these processes make a message feel less overtly persuasive and heig=
hten acceptance of the ideas it conveys.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-22" data-idx=3D"22">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">22</span>
      <h3><strong>Narratives and inoculation: revisiting a theoretical tens=
ion</strong></h3>
<p>Although narratives are widely valued for their persuasive potential, th=
eir role in inoculation remains unsettled. Scholarship suggests a dual role=
 (Compton &amp; Mason, 2020). Narrative features such as characters' person=
al experiences can make threat and refutation more concrete and memorable, =
potentially strengthening resistance (Lee et al., 2016); yet story immersio=
n may simultaneously suppress the counterarguing on which inoculation depen=
ds, as absorbed audiences grow less inclined to scrutinize claims or genera=
te opposing thoughts (Green &amp; Brock,  2000; Moyer-Gus=C3=A9, 2008; Moye=
r-Gus=C3=A9 &amp; Nabi, 2010). This dual role creates an apparent tension w=
ith inoculation's core function: inoculation protects attitudes by supplyin=
g weakened counterpositions and refutations that recipients can mobilize wh=
en their attitudes are later challenged (McGuire, 1964), so counterarguing =
is integral to its operation=E2=80=94yet the very immersion that makes narr=
ative compelling may blunt that process.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-23" data-idx=3D"23">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">23</span>
      <p>This tension, however, may reflect an overlooked conceptual distin=
ction. The counterarguing that narratives suppress is directed at the messa=
ge immediately encountered at that moment, whereas inoculation concerns res=
istance to a later attack, not to the inoculation message itself. Seen this=
 way, narrative immersion need not conflict with inoculation. If lowered re=
sistance to the inoculation message helps recipients absorb and retain its =
refutational content, their defenses may be reinforced when the attack arri=
ves. Existing evidence speaks only partly to this possibility. Inoculation =
messages occasionally incorporate narrative elements, such as dramatized re=
futations, to enhance vividness and engagement (Compton &amp; Mason, 2020),=
 but message format has seldom been treated as a design feature of inoculat=
ion in its own right. More often, narrative and inoculation are examined as=
 separate strategies: Niederdeppe et al. (2015), for example, found that na=
rrative messages increased policy support immediately after exposure, where=
as inoculation messages better protected attitudes a week later, and Geegan=
 et al.  (2023) found greater transportation and identification for narrati=
ve messages but stronger delayed resistance for inoculation messages. Infor=
mative as they are, such comparisons cannot show whether inoculation itself=
 operates differently when its core components are delivered in narrative v=
ersus expository form.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-24" data-idx=3D"24">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">24</span>
      <h3><strong>Toward narrative inoculation</strong></h3>
<p>Recent work suggests that the facilitative side of this dual-role accoun=
t may, under some conditions, emerge in practice. Biddlestone et al. (2023)=
, for instance, found that narrative-based <span id=3D"page-3-0"></span>ino=
culation increased resistance to conjunction errors, reducing conspiracy be=
liefs and improving truth discernment. Narratives, on this evidence, may in=
stantiate rather than obstruct inoculation, furnishing a concrete vehicle t=
hrough which threat and refutation are experienced and sustained. Several f=
eatures of narrative make this theoretically plausible. By embedding counte=
rarguments in character-driven episodes, narratives can dramatize faulty re=
asoning, model refutations, and make abstract claims more concrete. Compare=
d with expository inoculation, narrative formats may reduce psychological d=
istance by situating threat and refutation within relatable characters and =
experiences (Compton &amp; Mason, 2020). In doing so, they may make both vu=
lnerabilities to challenge and the logic of resistance more accessible. Mor=
eover, some evidence suggests that narratives can evoke anticipatory threat=
 even when forewarning is less explicit (Niederdeppe et al., 2015), indicat=
ing that narrative form may activate parts of the inoculation process.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-25" data-idx=3D"25">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">25</span>
      <p>Nevertheless, evidence from a direct comparison of message formats=
 for delivering inoculation's core components remains sparse. The present s=
tudy addresses this gap by comparing narrative and non-narrative inoculatio=
n messages that hold the inoculation elements constant while varying whethe=
r they are delivered in story-based or expository form. We further examine =
transportation and identification as potential mediators, since narrative e=
ngagement may not operate uniformly in resistance contexts (Compton &amp; M=
ason, 2020). This design permits a test not only of whether narrative inocu=
lation works, but also of whether these narrative mechanisms account for an=
y differences between formats. The following research questions are therefo=
re posed:</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-26" data-idx=3D"26">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">26</span>
      <p>RQ1: How will narrative inoculation differ from nonnarrative inocu=
lation in influencing (a) counterarguing against attack, (b) cancer fatalis=
m, (c) perceived efficacy in cancer prevention, and (d) intentions to adopt=
 cancer riskreduction behaviors?</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-27" data-idx=3D"27">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">27</span>
      <p>RQ2: How will (a) identification and (b) transportation mediate th=
e effects of narrative inoculation (vs. non-narrative inoculation)?</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-28" data-idx=3D"28">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">28</span>
      <h1><strong>Prophylactic and therapeutic inoculation</strong></h1>
<p>A recent development in inoculation theory extends its function to "trea=
t" individuals who already hold beliefs opposed to the advocated position (=
Compton, 2020). Traditionally, inoculation has been prophylactic, akin to v=
accination, designed to build resistance in healthy individuals before cont=
racting the pathogen. Classic studies accordingly screened participants bef=
orehand to confirm an initial position and then delivered messages that rei=
nforced it (e.g., Ivanov et al., 2009). The baseline attitude served as a p=
recondition rather than a variable of interest=E2=80=94what mattered was th=
at a defensible position existed prior to inoculation.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-29" data-idx=3D"29">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">29</span>
      <p>In this paradigm, inoculation defends an established attitude agai=
nst persuasive attack. Its forewarning component presumes a preexisting bel=
ief to protect; absent such an anchor, perceived threat weakens, and so doe=
s the motivation to defend that belief. This dependence on an initial attit=
udinal position has long been recognized as a boundary condition of the the=
ory. Prophylactic inoculation thus targets recipients in a "healthy" cognit=
ive state, aiming to prevent attitude change. In the context of fatalism, p=
rophylactic inoculation would serve to maintain non-fatalistic beliefs: it =
would work to keep non-fatalists non-fatalistic. Yet health interventions u=
nfold in settings where audiences differ widely in their prior beliefs, rai=
sing a critical question: if recipients already hold beliefs contrary to th=
e inoculation message, can inoculation shift their position in the advocate=
d direction?</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-30" data-idx=3D"30">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">30</span>
      <p>To address this, scholars have turned to therapeutic inoculation, =
the analogue of therapeutic vaccination in medicine, administered after an =
individual has already been "infected" with a harmful belief (Eggermont, 20=
09). This line of research asks whether inoculation can correct or mitigate=
 already-formed beliefs rather than merely prevent their formation. Wood (2=
007) was among the first to test this possibility directly. In this work on=
 agricultural biotechnology, inoculation strengthened positive attitudes to=
ward the advocated position, particularly among those initially opposed. No=
tably, participants with neutral or opposing attitudes perceived greater th=
reat from the inoculation message than did supportive participants, hinting=
 at therapeutic inoculation's promise.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-31" data-idx=3D"31">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">31</span>
      <p>Subsequent research provides evidence that inoculation can operate=
 across the attitudinal spectrum. Ivanov et al. (2017) found that it shifte=
d attitudes even among those initially neutral or opposed to certain touris=
t destinations, with the shifts persisting after counter-attitudinal attack=
s. Similarly, van der Linden et al. (2017) showed that inoculation messages=
 emphasizing the scientific consensus on climate change both reinforced pro=
-science attitudes and reduced skepticism among those prone to doubt. Mason=
 et al. (2024) extended the pattern to conspiracy beliefs, documenting effe=
cts that were at once prophylactic and therapeutic: inoculation bolstered r=
esistance among those with lower baseline conspiracist beliefs while reduci=
ng such beliefs among those with higher predispositions.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-32" data-idx=3D"32">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">32</span>
      <p>These findings carry practical implications for fatalism intervent=
ions, where the goal is not only to shield non-fatalists from adopting fata=
listic beliefs but also to correct those who already hold them. In today's =
fragmented and often contradictory information environment, anyone may be v=
ulnerable to fatalistic thinking, making it essential that real-world messa=
ge strategies engage diverse audiences simultaneously, functioning prophyla=
ctically and therapeutically, to benefit all recipients regardless of basel=
ine positions. As Compton and Ivanov (2013) note, inoculation can do both: =
reinforcing supportive attitudes while shifting neutral or opposed position=
s toward the advocated direction. This dual applicability positions inocula=
tion as a generalizable strategy for building resilience across the full sp=
ectrum of belief levels. The following hypotheses are therefore proposed:</=
p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-33" data-idx=3D"33">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">33</span>
      <p>H2: Among individuals lower in baseline cancer fatalism, inoculate=
d participants will report less belief change counter to the advocated posi=
tion (i.e., maintained low levels of cancer fatalism) than control particip=
ants.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-34" data-idx=3D"34">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">34</span>
      <p><span id=3D"page-4-0"></span>H3: Among individuals higher in basel=
ine cancer fatalism, inoculated participants will report greater belief cha=
nge toward the advocated position (i.e., reduced cancer fatalism) than cont=
rol participants.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-35" data-idx=3D"35">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">35</span>
      <h1><strong>Method</strong></h1>
<p>This study used a two-phase online experiment with Singaporean adults re=
cruited through Dynata's online panel. Asian populations are considered mor=
e strongly influenced by cancer fatalism than other groups (Chen &amp; Kim,=
 2024). Among Asian societies, Singapore offers a particularly suitable set=
ting: its multiethnic population, comprising primarily Chinese, Malay, and =
Indian communities, provides a rich cultural backdrop for studying cancer f=
atalism. Its relatively high prevalence of fatalistic beliefs and low uptak=
e of preventive behaviors (Kim &amp; Lwin,  2017), together with limited ec=
onomic barriers to health promotion and high rates of Internet penetration =
and news consumption, make it well suited for examining how communication s=
hapes cancer control. In total, 572 participants completed Phase 1 of the e=
xperiment, and 332 completed the follow-up two weeks later (58.0% retention=
). Sociodemographic characteristics are reported in <a href=3D"http://local=
host:8080/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#supplementary-data">Table =
S1</a>.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-36" data-idx=3D"36">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">36</span>
      <h3><strong>Stimuli and pre-test</strong></h3>
<p>All three Phase 1 stimuli began with an overview of the cancer informati=
on landscape; the two inoculation messages then added inoculation component=
s. The non-narrative message used a conventional expository format, forewar=
ning participants about the threat posed by conflicting information and its=
 potential to foster cancer fatalism, then preemptively refuting anticipate=
d counterarguments with evidence affirming the value of cancer prevention. =
The narrative message conveyed the same content through a first-person stor=
y. Conflicting cancer information in the media initially left the protagoni=
st confused and pessimistic (forewarning), but, guided by a doctor, the pro=
tagonist learned to verify claims and adopt a healthier lifestyle (refutati=
onal preemption). Ultimately, the protagonist felt less fatalistic, more al=
ert to conflicting information, more confident about reducing cancer risk, =
and healthier overall.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-minor" id=3D"para-37" data-idx=3D"37"=
>
      <span class=3D"para-idx">37</span>
      <p>A pre-test (<em>N</em> =3D 210) confirmed that, as intended, the i=
noculation messages elicited greater perceived threat than the control mess=
age. Participants were randomly assigned to one of the three conditions, an=
d a one-way ANOVA revealed a significant difference in elicited threat, <em=
>F</em>(2, 207) =3D 4.23, <em>p</em> =3D .016, <em>=CE=B7</em>2 =3D .04. Po=
st hoc comparisons indicated that both the non-narrative inoculation (<em>n=
</em> =3D 71, <em>M</em> =3D 5.81, <em>SD</em> =3D 0.69, <em>SE</em> =3D .1=
3, <em>p</em> =3D .049) and the narrative inoculation (<em>n</em> =3D 69, <=
em>M</em> =3D 5.85, <em>SD</em> =3D 0.68, <em>SE</em> =3D .13, <em>p</em> =
=3D .024) elicited significantly higher threat than the control group (<em>=
n</em> =3D 70, <em>M</em> =3D 5.51, <em>SD</em> =3D 0.87), validating the m=
anipulation.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-38" data-idx=3D"38">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">38</span>
      <p>The attack messages used to challenge participants' beliefs featur=
ed conflicting information about cancer prevention. Such information refers=
 to messages about a single health behavior that present different or oppos=
ing claims, or that contain logically inconsistent assertions, thereby crea=
ting uncertainty about what is accurate and what course of action to take (=
Nagler &amp;  LoRusso, 2017). The present attack messages were designed to =
embody this form of informational and decisional conflict.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-39" data-idx=3D"39">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">39</span>
      <p>In Phase 2, participants read an attack message presenting conflic=
ting scientific evidence on one of two cancer-prevention themes (red meat c=
onsumption or hair dye use). Each message first cited research suggesting a=
 cancer risk, then contrasted it with conflicting findings, shifting conclu=
sions, or alternative explanations. This juxtaposition of possible risk and=
 unresolved uncertainty created ambiguity about the behavioral implications=
, mirroring the conflicting cancer information people routinely encounter i=
n public discourse.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-40" data-idx=3D"=
40">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">40</span>
      <p>Each attack message also closed with brief fatalistic statements. =
These were intended not as the central persuasive claim but as a simulation=
 of natural "antigenic exposure," a feature central to this study and consi=
stent with the medical analogy underlying inoculation theory (McGuire, 1961=
, 1964). They reflected the interpretive leap that often follows scientific=
 uncertainty, whereby unresolved evidence invites resignation rather than a=
ction.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-41" data-idx=3D"=
41">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">41</span>
      <p>Although theme comparisons were not a focus of the study, using bo=
th themes added variety and strengthened the robustness of the inoculation =
test. Independent-samples t-tests revealed no significant differences betwe=
en themes in perceived message credibility, source credibility, perceived e=
fficacy, counterarguing, or cancer fatalism. Given this statistical equival=
ence, the two conditions were combined for analysis. All stimuli are availa=
ble in <a href=3D"http://localhost:8080/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag=
018#supplementary-data">Supplementary Appendix A</a>.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-42" data-idx=3D"42">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">42</span>
      <h3><strong>Main experiment procedures</strong></h3>
<p>The two-phase experiment was conducted online. In Phase 1, participants =
completed baseline measures of cancer fatalism and issue involvement before=
 being randomly assigned to read a narrative inoculation message, a non-nar=
rative inoculation message, or a control message. Immediately after exposur=
e, their threat perceptions, identification, transportation, perceived effi=
cacy, behavioral intentions, and postintervention fatalism were assessed. T=
wo weeks later, in Phase 2, participants received an attack message contain=
ing conflicting cancer information; their counterarguments were recorded, f=
ollowed by final assessments of cancer fatalism, perceived efficacy, and in=
tentions to adopt cancer risk-reduction behaviors.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-43" data-idx=3D"43">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">43</span>
      <h3><strong>Measurements</strong></h3>
<p><a href=3D"http://localhost:8080/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#=
supplementary-data">Table S2</a> reports the descriptive statistics and cor=
relations for study variables, and <a href=3D"http://localhost:8080/article=
-lookup/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#supplementary-data">Table S3</a> presents t=
he full wording of all measurement items.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-44" data-idx=3D"=
44">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">44</span>
      <h3><strong>Elicited threat</strong></h3>
<p>Elicited threat was assessed separately for conflicting cancer informati=
on and cancer fatalism. Consistent with recent developments in inoculation =
research, threat was operationalized as two related but distinct perception=
s: apprehensive threat and motivational threat (e.g., Banas &amp; Richards,=
 2017). Apprehensive threat captures the extent to which a challenge is per=
ceived as unsettling or threatening, and motivational threat reflects the e=
xtent to which that challenge activates a defensive motivation and readines=
s to resist (Banas &amp; Richards, 2017). After Phase 1 exposure, apprehens=
ive threat was measured with five semantic differential items on 7-point sc=
ales (e.g., 1 =3D <em>nonthreatening</em>, <span id=3D"page-5-0"></span>7 =
=3D threatening), with reliability of  <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax Ctxt=
