    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-52" data-idx=3D"52">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">52</span>
      <h3><strong>Results</strong></h3>
<h4><strong>Analytical approach</strong></h4>
<p>All analyses were conducted in SPSS v.27. Chi-square tests revealed no s=
ignificant differences across conditions in age, gender, ethnicity, educati=
on, household income, or personal and family cancer history (see Table S1),=
 consistent with successful randomization; these sociodemographic variables=
 were therefore not included as covariates.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-53" data-idx=3D"=
53">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">53</span>
      <p>To test the comparative effects of the experimental conditions, se=
parate one-way multivariate analyses of covariance (MANCOVA) were conducted=
 for the Phase 1 and Phase 2 outcomes, using the respective analytic sample=
s (N=3D572 in Phase 1; n=3D332 in Phase 2). Baseline cancer fatalism and is=
sue involvement served as covariates, and condition (control, non-narrative=
 inoculation, or narrative inoculation) served as the between-subjects fact=
or. Two orthogonal planned Helmert contrasts followed up the omnibus effect=
 of condition. The first contrast (C1) compared the control condition with =
the two inoculation conditions combined, directly testing H1 that inoculati=
on would yield more favorable outcomes than control, and the second contras=
t (C2) compared the two inoculation conditions, addressing RQ1 on whether t=
he narrative and non-narrative formats differed in their effects.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-54" data-idx=3D"=
54">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">54</span>
      <p>Mediation and moderation analyses used the PROCESS macro for SPSS.=
 To address RQ2, the mediation model was estimated on the inoculation-only =
subsample (non-narrative =3D 1, narrative =3D 2), since it sought to explai=
n differences between the two inoculation formats rather than the overall i=
noculation-versuscontrol effect. Parallel mediation was tested with Model 4=
, specifying identification and transportation as simultaneous mediators an=
d issue involvement as a covariate; indirect effects were estimated via ord=
inary least squares (OLS) regression with 5,000 bootstrap resamples and 95%=
 percentile confidence intervals. To test whether inoculation effects varie=
d with baseline fatalism (H2 and H3), moderation was examined using Model 1=
, with baseline fatalism and issue involvement mean-centered. Experimental =
condition was coded control =3D 1 versus inoculation =3D 2, and significant=
 interactions were probed using the Johnson-Neyman technique (Johnson &amp;=
 Fay, 1950).</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-55" data-idx=3D"55">
