eat measures: apprehensive threat toward conflicting cancer information, <e=
m>F</em>(2, 567) =3D 3.44, <em>p</em> =3D .033, <em>=CE=B7</em><sup>2</sup>=
 <em>p</em> =3D .01, and cancer fatalism, <em>F</em>(2, 567) =3D 4.84, <em>=
p =3D</em> .008, <em>=CE=B7</em><sup>2</sup> <em><sup>p</sup></em>=3D .02; =
motivational threat toward conflicting cancer information, <em>F</em>(2, 56=
7) =3D 9.51, <em>p &lt;</em> .001, <em>=CE=B7</em><sup>2</sup> <em>p</em> =
=3D .03, and cancer fatalism, <em>F</em>(2, 567) =3D 10.80, <em>p &lt;</em>=
 .001, <em>=CE=B7</em>2 <em>p</em> =3D .04. Helmert contrast C1 (see Table =
1) indicated that, relative to control, participants in the inoculation con=
ditions reported significantly higher apprehensive threat toward both confl=
icting cancer information (estimate =3D <em>=E2=88=92</em>.28, <em>SE</em> =
=3D .11, <em>p</em> =3D .012) and cancer fatalism (estimate =3D<em>=E2=88=
=92</em>.30, <em>SE</em> =3D .10, <em>p</em> =3D .004), as well as higher m=
otivational threat toward both conflicting cancer information (estimate =3D=
<em>=E2=88=92</em>.35, <em>SE</em> =3D .08, <em>p &lt;</em> .001) and cance=
r fatalism (estimate =3D<em>=E2=88=92</em>.38, <em>SE</em> =3D .08, <em>p &=
lt;* .001). Helmert contrast C2 showed no significant differences between t=
he two inoculation conditions on any threat measure (all *p*s *&gt;</em> .2=
4), indicating that narrative and non-narrative formats were equivalent in =
eliciting threat (also see <a href=3D"http://localhost:8080/article-lookup/=
doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#supplementary-data">Table S4</a>)). In sum, inocula=
tion strategies elevated threat perceptions relative to control as intended=
.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-58" data-idx=3D"58">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">58</span>
      <h2><strong>Traditional inoculation outcomes</strong></h2>
<p>Univariate analyses revealed significant effects of condition on postint=
ervention fatalism, <em>F</em>(2, 567) =3D 6.12, <em>p</em> =3D .002, <em>=
=CE=B7</em><sup>2</sup> <em><sup>p</sup></em>=3D .02; postattack fatalism, =
<em>F</em>(2, 327) =3D 5.10, <em>p</em> =3D .007, <em>=CE=B7</em><sup>2</su=
p> <em>p</em>=3D .03; postintervention efficacy, <em>F</em>(2, 567) =3D 4.6=
7, <em>p</em> =3D .010, <em>=CE=B7</em><sup>2</sup> <em><sup>p</sup></em>=
=3D .02; postattack efficacy, <em>F</em>(2, 327) =3D 7.11, <em>p &lt;</em> =
.001, <em>=CE=B7</em><sup>2</sup> <em><sup>p</sup></em>=3D .04; and only Ph=
ase 1 alcohol avoidance intention, <em>F</em>(2, 567) =3D 4.13, <em>p =3D</=
em> .017, <em>=CE=B7</em><sup>2</sup> <em><sup>p</sup></em>=3D .01, among t=
he behavioral intentions. The effect on counterarguing against the attack m=
essage was not significant, <em>F</em>(2, 327) =3D 2.62, <em>p =3D</em> .07=
5, <em>=CE=B7</em><sup>2</sup> <em><sup>p</sup></em>=3D .02.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-59" data-idx=3D"59">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">59</span>
      <p>Helmert contrast C1 (see Table 1) showed that, compared with contr=
ol, inoculated participants reported lower fatalism and higher perceived ef=
ficacy at both postintervention (fatalism: estimate =3D .25, <em>SE</em> =
=3D .07, <em>p &lt;</em> .001; efficacy: estimate =3D<em>=E2=88=92</em>.25,=
 <em>SE</em> =3D .08, <em>p</em> =3D .002) and postattack (fatalism: estima=
te =3D .28, <em>SE</em> =3D .09, <em>p</em> =3D .002; efficacy: estimate =
=3D<em>=E2=88=92</em>.42, <em>SE</em> =3D .11, <em>p &lt;</em> .001), suppo=
rting H1b and H1c. The contrast was not significant for counterarguing agai=
nst attack (estimate =3D .11, <em>SE</em> =3D .14, <em>p</em> =3D .438); H1=
a was not supported. Inoculated participants also reported stronger intenti=
ons to consume fruit and vegetables (estimate =3D <em>=E2=88=92</em>.25, <e=
m>SE</em> =3D .11, <em>p</em> =3D .029) and avoid alcohol (estimate =3D<em>=
=E2=88=92</em>.35, <em>SE</em> =3D .12, <em>p</em> =3D .005) in Phase 1, an=
d to engage in physical activity in Phase 2 (estimate =3D<em>=E2=88=92</em>=
.39, <em>SE</em> =3D .17, <em>p</em> =3D .019), partially supporting H1d.</=
p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-60" data-idx=3D"60">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">60</span>
      <h3><strong>Narrative vs. non-narrative inoculation</strong></h3>
<p>Helmert contrast C2 (see Table 1) revealed no significant differences be=
tween the non-narrative inoculation and narrative inoculation conditions in=
 identification (estimate =3D <em>=E2=88=92</em>.13, <em>SE</em> =3D .10, <=
em>p</em> =3D .205) or transportation (estimate =3D<em>=E2=88=92</em>.06, <=
em>SE</em> =3D .10, <em>p</em> =3D .547). Although the combined inoculation=
 conditions did not differ significantly from control in counterarguing, pa=
rticipants in the narrative inoculation condition reported greater countera=
rguing against the subsequent attack than those in the non-narrative inocul=
ation condition (estimate =3D <em>=E2=88=92</em>.36, <em>SE</em> =3D .17, <=
em>p</em> =3D .031), addressing RQ1a. No significant differences between th=
e narrative and non-narrative inoculation conditions emerged for cancer fat=
alism (RQ1b), perceived efficacy (RQ1c), or behavioral intentions (RQ1d). C=
ovariate-adjusted Dunnett comparisons showed that each format produced a si=
milar pattern of significant favorable outcomes relative to control (see <a=
 href=3D"http://localhost:8080/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#suppl=
ementary-data">Table S4)</a>. Neither format consistently outperformed the =
other.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-61" data-idx=3D"61">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">61</span>
      <p>For RQ2, the mediation model showed that narrative inoculation gen=
erated higher counterarguing against the attack (<em>b</em> =3D .37, <em>SE=
</em> =3D .16, <em>t</em> =3D 2.25, <em>p</em> =3D .026, 95% CI [.05, .69])=
, consistent with Helmert contrast C2. However, none of the indirect effect=
s through identification or transportation were significant; all bootstrap =
confidence intervals included zero. Thus, neither narrative engagement mech=
anism mediated the effect on counterarguing.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-62" data-idx=3D"62">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">62</span>
      <h3><strong>Prophylactic vs. therapeutic inoculation</strong></h3>
<p>Postintervention change in cancer fatalism (postintervention minus basel=
ine) was regressed on experimental condition (control vs. inoculation), bas=
eline fatalism, and their interaction, controlling for issue involvement (s=
ee <a href=3D"http://localhost:8080/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#=
supplementary-data">Table S5)</a>. The interaction was significant (<em>b</=
em> =3D <em>=E2=88=92</em>.15, <em>SE</em> =3D .06, <em>t</em> =3D <em>=E2=
=88=92<em>2.31, <em>p</em> =3D .021, 95% CI [</em>=E2=88=92</em>.27, <em>=
=E2=88=92</em>.02]), indicating that the inoculation effect strengthened as=
 baseline fatalism increased. The Johnson=E2=80=93Neyman technique identifi=
ed a transition point of 3.49. The two conditions did not differ significan=
tly below this value of baseline fatalism, whereas inoculation significantl=
y reduced fatalism relative to the control condition above it, encompassing=
 approximately 74% of the sample (see <a href=3D"http://localhost:8080/arti=
cle-lookup/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#supplementary-data">Table S6</a> and Fig=
ure 1).</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-63" data-idx=3D"=
63">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">63</span>
      <p>To interpret this region in relation to H2 and H3, the midpoint of=
 the 7-point scale (4 =3D neither agree nor disagree) served as a reference=
. Following prior research that treated a neutral response as the boundary =
between preexisting positions (e.g., Ivanov et al., 2017; Wood, 2007), scor=
es of 4 or below denoted lower baseline fatalism, whereas scores above 4 de=
noted higher baseline fatalism. These ranges correspond, respectively, to t=
he comparatively "healthy" state relevant to prophylactic inoculation and t=
he "already affected" state relevant to therapeutic inoculation. As expecte=
d, participants in the lower-fatalism range (<em>n</em> =3D 248, <em>M</em>=
 =3D 3.05, <em>SD</em> =3D 0.79) scored significantly lower than those in t=
he higher-fatalism range (<em>n</em> =3D 324, <em>M</em> =3D 4.89, <em>SD</=
em> =3D 0.63), <em>t</em>(465) =3D *=E2=88=92*30.09, <em>p &lt;</em> .001. =
Because the Johnson=E2=80=93Neyman transition point (3.49) fell below 4, th=
e significant effect included the entire higher-fatalism range, supporting =
H3 at postintervention and indicating an immediate therapeutic effect. It a=
lso extended into the upper portion of the lower-fatalism range (3.49=E2=80=
=934.00), consistent with a prophylactic function of preserving or reinforc=
ing comparatively low fatalism. Because the effect did not extend below 3.4=
9, H2 received partial support at postintervention.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-64" data-idx=3D"64">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">64</span>
      <p>A parallel analysis examined postattack change in cancer fatalism =
(postattack minus baseline). The interaction was not significant (<em>b</em=
> =3D<em>=E2=88=92</em>.11, <em>SE</em> =3D .08, <em>t</em> =3D<em>=E2=88=
=92<em>1.35, <em>p</em> =3D .176, 95% CI [</em>=E2=88=92</em>.27, .05]). Ex=
ploratory Johnson=E2=80=93Neyman probing nevertheless identified a similar =
transition point of 3.37 (see <a href=3D"http://localhost:8080/article-look=
up/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#supplementary-data">Table S7</a> and Figure 1), =
with the estimated inoculation effect being nonsignificant below this basel=
ine fatalism value and significant above it. This region</p>

