    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-68" data-idx=3D"68">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">68</span>
      <p><span id=3D"page-8-0"></span><img src=3D"http://localhost:8080/_pa=
ge_8_Figure_4.jpeg" alt=3D""></p>
<p><img src=3D"http://localhost:8080/_page_8_Figure_5.jpeg" alt=3D""></p>
<p><strong>Figure 1</strong> Johnson=E2=80=93Neyman plot.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-69" data-idx=3D"69">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">69</span>
      <p>again included the entire higher-fatalism range and the upper port=
ion of the lower-fatalism range. Given the nonsignificant interaction, howe=
ver, this pattern did not provide confirmatory evidence that the therapeuti=
c and partial prophylactic effects persisted after the attack. In summary, =
the hypotheses received support only at postintervention: H2 received parti=
al support, whereas H3 was supported. Descriptive statistics for fatalism c=
hanges across conditions and phases by baseline levels are provided in <a h=
ref=3D"http://localhost:8080/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/joc/jqag018#supplem=
entary-data">Table S8.</a></p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-70" data-idx=3D"70">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">70</span>
      <h1><strong>Discussion</strong></h1>
<p>This study examined how inoculation messages, delivered in narrative and=
 non-narrative formats, shape resistance to conflicting information and fat=
alistic beliefs. Relative to the control, both formats reduced fatalistic b=
eliefs and heightened perceived efficacy, with modest effects on risk-reduc=
tion intentions. These results indicate the value of inoculation for counte=
ring the adverse effects of contradictory messages and underscore the <span=
 id=3D"page-9-0"></span>flexibility of the theory, extending its relevance =
beyond the attitude protection of traditional persuasion to settings in whi=
ch conflicting information erodes confidence in action.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-71" data-idx=3D"71">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">71</span>
      <p>A central implication of this study is that message format may mat=
ter less for inoculation than the preservation of its core structure. Altho=
ugh inoculation research has closely examined threat and refutational preem=
ption, less attention has been given to whether these elements operate diff=
erently across narrative and non-narrative formats. This gap is notable giv=
en narrative's ambivalent role in resistance processes (Compton &amp; Mason=
, 2020). Comparing two messages that shared the same inoculation logic but =
differed in format, we found that both elicited threat and supported resist=
ance, and that neither consistently outperformed the other. Inoculation's e=
ffectiveness may thus depend less on format than on whether its essential e=
lements are successfully conveyed.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-72" data-idx=3D"72">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">72</span>
      <p>The findings also refine how the role of narrative in inoculation =
should be understood. One concern is that narrative may undermine inoculati=
on by reducing the critical processing that resistance requires (e.g., Comp=
ton &amp; Ivanov, 2013; Compton &amp; Mason, 2020). Yet the elevated post-a=
ttack counterarguing observed in the narrative condition suggests that what=
ever reduction in critical processing narrative may produce at initial inoc=
ulation exposure need not translate into weaker defensive responding when a=
n attack later arrives. This pattern should nevertheless be interpreted wit=
h caution, as narrative inoculation did not produce significantly greater c=
ounterarguing than the non-inoculation control and thus cannot establish th=
at narrative delivery enhanced resistance in absolute terms. What it does i=
ndicate is a format-specific difference between the two inoculation conditi=
ons: narrative delivery was more conducive than non-narrative delivery to t=
his later resistance response. One possibility is that story-based presenta=
tion may help recipients encode or retain refutational material in ways tha=
t become useful at the point of attack (Appel &amp; Richter, 2007). Althoug=
h the present study cannot directly test this possibility, the results sugg=
est that narrative is not inherently detrimental to inoculation and may, un=
der some conditions, complement its defensive logic.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-73" data-idx=3D"=
73">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">73</span>
      <p>These results also speak to a related concern that narrative frami=
ng may narrow the umbrella of protection classic inoculation affords by bin=
ding resistance too tightly to particular characters or episodes, limiting =
its transfer to novel attacks (Compton &amp; Mason, 2020). Although our des=
ign did not test breadth of protection across attack contexts, narrative in=
oculation was no less effective than its non-narrative counterpart on the o=
utcomes assessed. Narrative delivery, in other words, appears not to have c=
ompromised inoculation's protective function, even if it conferred no consi=
stent advantage. What may matter, then, is not whether a message is narrati=
ve, but how narrative elements are integrated with inoculation's core compo=
nents in the service of defensive preparation.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-74" data-idx=3D"74">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">74</span>
      <p>The mediation results reinforce this interpretation. Transportatio=
n and identification did not account for the observed effects, consistent w=
ith evidence that these mechanisms are neither automatic nor uniformly bene=
ficial in resistance contexts (Compton &amp; Mason, 2020). They may be bett=
er understood as contingent audience responses than as defining criteria of=
 narrative, with persuasive implications that vary across genre, length, me=
dium, and character (Ratcliff &amp; Sun, 2020); comparable work likewise fi=
nds that transportation and identification did not translate into stronger =
resistance to a delayed attack (Geegan et al., 2023). The absence of mediat=
ion here suggests that narrative engagement, at least as indexed by transpo=
rtation and identification, may be insufficient on its own to strengthen in=
oculation. Its value may instead hinge on whether specific narrative featur=
es support inoculation's core architecture, for example through character d=
evelopment, emotional flow, or plot structure (Busselle &amp; Bilandzic, 20=
09). Toward this theoretical direction, future research should therefore mo=
ve beyond asking whether narrative is better or worse for inoculation to in=
stead examine what kinds of narrative experiences meaningfully shape inocul=
ation processes, identifying when narrative format alters not merely how in=
oculation looks, but how it works.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-75" data-idx=3D"75">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">75</span>
      <p>Beyond message formats, this study offers preliminary evidence tha=
t inoculation may serve both prophylactic and therapeutic functions dependi=
ng on recipients' preexisting belief. In its classic prophylactic form, ino=
culation preserves resistance in advance of a challenge rather than initiat=
ing change (Compton, 2020; Wood, 2007). However, the present findings speak=
 more to its therapeutic counterpart. Among participants higher in baseline=
 fatalism, inoculation immediately reduced fatalistic beliefs relative to c=
ontrol, suggesting that the strategy may do more than shield existing belie=
fs. When conditions permit change, it may also disrupt maladaptive interpre=
tations already in place and shift established beliefs (Ivanov et al., 2017=
; Wood, 2007). This extension of inoculation beyond protection to correctio=
n is particularly valuable given how difficult fatalistic beliefs can be to=
 reverse once uncertainty has been internalized as evidence that prevention=
 is futile.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-76" data-idx=3D"=
76">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">76</span>
      <p>Although the therapeutic evidence was clearer immediately after th=
e intervention, the postattack interaction was nonsignificant. The findings=
 therefore support immediate belief reduction but not a durable therapeutic=
 effect. The inoculation effect also extended somewhat below the theoretica=
l midpoint, reaching some participants whose baseline fatalism was comparat=
ively low. This pattern is compatible with prophylactic potential, but an i=
mmediate reduction in fatalism does not, by itself, demonstrate protection =
against a subsequent attack. Whether inoculation also prophylactically prot=
ects these recipients from becoming more fatalistic following such a challe=
nge remains a question for future research.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-77" data-idx=3D"77">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">77</span>
      <p>This tentative prophylactic=E2=80=93therapeutic functionality can =
be situated within prior work linking inoculation with efficacy. Pfau et al=
. (2009) held that threat highlights vulnerability, while refutational pree=
mption builds confidence in defending attitudes. Jackson et al. (2015) exte=
nded this logic beyond attitudes, showing that inoculation can protect self=
-efficacy under challenge. Together, this work suggests that the present in=
tervention may operate not only by prompting counterarguing but also by bol=
stering confidence in managing conflicting information=E2=80=94an important=
 potential mechanism, since cancer fatalism is marked by diminished confide=
nce in preventive action. This interpretation accords with emerging theory =
that inoculation is not purely cognitive: its threat component carries emot=
ional implications and may interact with efficacy to shape resistance (Comp=
ton et al., 2022). More specifically, theorization linking inoculation and =
fear-and-efficacy processes suggests that resistance may be strongest when =
recipients perceive both vulnerability and coping capacity (Pfau et al., 20=
09). Where conflicting information evokes the uncertainty and helplessness =
that feed fatalistic <span id=3D"page-10-0"></span>interpretations, inocula=
tion may help reframe anticipated challenges as manageable, potentially pre=
serving a less fatalistic orientation while also enabling belief correction=
.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-78" data-idx=3D"78">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">78</span>
      <p>Taken together, the findings suggest that recipients' level of pre=
existing belief may condition their responses to inoculation, but they do n=
ot establish equally strong prophylactic and therapeutic effects across the=
 full range of baseline beliefs. A single inoculation message may neverthel=
ess reach audiences at different stages of preexisting belief without requi=
ring entirely separate tailoring to each audience segment (Compton, 2020), =
although the uneven effects caution against assuming that one approach will=
 benefit all recipients equally. More broadly, the findings illustrate the =
dual character of media effects as a source of maladaptive beliefs and a re=
source for resisting them, especially in communicating health, risk, and ot=
her domains marked by conflicting information. Given the modest effect size=
s, singleexposure design, and nonsignificant postattack interaction, these =
observations remain preliminary. Future research should examine whether rep=
eated exposure to inoculation messages produces more durable prophylactic a=
nd therapeutic effects.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-79" data-idx=3D"79">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">79</span>
      <p>Finally, although the inoculation affected fatalism and efficacy f=
airly consistently, it increased only intentions to consume fruits and vege=
tables and avoid alcohol in Phase 1 and to engage in physical activity in P=
hase 2. Inoculation, then, does not appear to enhance preventive intentions=
 broadly; rather, it may influence the proximal cognitive antecedents of pr=
evention more reliably than downstream intentions, which likely depend on t=
he nature of the behavior, assessment timing, and context. Future work shou=
ld examine when reduced fatalism and greater efficacy translate into durabl=
e intentions and, ultimately, behavioral change across domains, using behav=
ioral follow-ups and repeated assessment over longer intervals.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-80" data-idx=3D"80">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">80</span>
      <p>The study has several limitations. It used a single message per co=
ndition and a shorter control message; this allowed an initial comparison o=
f narrative and non-narrative inoculation but leaves open whether the findi=
ngs are free of stimulus-specific confounds or generalizable beyond these m=
essages. Future research should employ multiple messages (e.g., Niederdeppe=
 et al., 2015) and length-matched controls while holding core inoculation e=
lements constant to support stronger inferences about narrative form. In ad=
dition, the small effect sizes warrant caution regarding practical signific=
ance. Such modest effects may be expected from a brief, single-exposure int=
ervention targeting change-resistant beliefs, but whether repeated exposure=
 would produce more durable change in real-world settings remains a questio=
n the current design cannot resolve and future research should explore. Fin=
ally, this study did not comprehensively test mechanisms. Meaningful narrat=
ives may prompt self-reflection, reduce selective avoidance, satisfy autono=
my needs, and lower reactance (Moyer-Gus=C3=A9 &amp; Wilson, 2024); whether=
 these processes explain narrative inoculation effects awaits investigation=
.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para severity-moderate" id=3D"para-81" data-idx=3D"=
81">
      <span class=3D"para-idx">81</span>
      <p>In conclusion, this study advances inoculation theory by extending=
 its scope beyond the preservation of discrete attitudes to the modificatio=
n of broader belief orientations and resistance to adverse media effects. I=
n the context of conflicting cancer information, both narrative and non-nar=
rative inoculation reduced fatalism and strengthened perceived efficacy, su=
ggesting that inoculation's protective capacity may rest less on message fo=
rmat than on the effective delivery of threat and refutational preemption. =
At the same time, the narrative format's association with greater postattac=
k counterarguing indicates that storytelling can complement, rather than co=
mpromise, defensive processing. Additionally, the findings point to possibl=
e prophylactic and therapeutic applications of inoculation, highlighting it=
s potential as a flexible strategy for fostering resilience amid informatio=
nal uncertainty.</p>

    </div><div class=3D"para" id=3D"para-82" data-idx=3D"82">
