   =20
  </div><div class=3D"comment-panel-content"><div class=3D"overall-feedback=
"><h3>Overall Feedback</h3><p>The paper=E2=80=99s opening is clear, well or=
ganized, and motivated by an important health-communication problem, with t=
he randomized comparison of narrative and non-narrative inoculation offerin=
g a potentially valuable contribution. Its strongest conceptual feature is =
the tension between narratives=E2=80=99 tendency to reduce resistance to th=
eir own persuasive content and inoculation=E2=80=99s reliance on counterarg=
uing against subsequent attacks; however, the framing should distinguish th=
ese targets more explicitly, since reduced counterarguing toward an inocula=
tion message need not imply reduced counterarguing toward later conflicting=
 information. The claimed extension from specific attitudes to broader beli=
ef orientations also needs sharper justification, including clearer distinc=
tions among fatalism, uncertainty, and perceived efficacy. Most importantly=
, the prophylactic=E2=80=93therapeutic interpretation requires evidence sep=
arating immediate persuasion from resistance following an attack: reduction=
s in initially high fatalism do not alone demonstrate inoculation, while ma=
intenance of low fatalism requires an appropriate comparison and attention =
to floor effects and regression to the mean. The substantial loss of partic=
ipants between phases likewise warrants transparent attrition analyses. Fin=
ally, the abstract should distinguish narrative superiority in counterargui=
ng from superiority in substantive outcomes and specify which behavioral in=
tentions improved, rather than using the vague phrase =E2=80=9Ccertain inte=
ntions to adopt certain risk-reduction behaviors.=E2=80=9D Overall, the int=
roduction establishes a promising study, but its theoretical claims require=
 more precise construct definitions and careful alignment with the experime=
ntal evidence.</p>
</div><div class=3D"section-label" style=3D"color:#92400e">Moderate Issues =
(13)</div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-=
sol_0" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_0">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Fatalistic belief is treated as defensive =
avoidance without establishing the distinction</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_0" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Fatalism is linked to low efficacy and function=
s as a form of defensive avoidance when individuals feel incapable of effec=
tive action</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The association between fatalism and low =
efficacy provides a reasonable basis for the proposed intervention. I initi=
ally read the defensive-avoidance account as a tentative mechanism, consist=
ent with the opening description of a =E2=80=9Cplausible mechanism.=E2=80=
=9D However, this later sentence presents that account as an established fu=
nction of fatalism. In fear-appeal frameworks, defensive avoidance is a res=
ponse to threatening information, whereas fatalism is a belief about the co=
ntrollability of outcomes. The two can be related without being equivalent:=
 fatalism could precede avoidance, result from it, or share antecedents wit=
h it. The surrounding discussion does not establish which relationship appl=
ies here. This matters because the claim that inoculation replaces avoidanc=
e with adaptive processing depends on more than the documented association =
between fatalism and low efficacy.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace the sentence with: =E2=80=9CFatalism is linked to low efficac=
y and may contribute to, or arise from, defensive avoidance when individual=
s feel incapable of effective action.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 18</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_2" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_2">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">The proposed test is described as demonstr=
ating cumulative and long-term protection</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_2" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Extending inoculation here tests whether threat=
 and counterarguing can fortify resistance not to a discrete persuasive att=
ack but to an orientation built up through repeated exposure to conflicting=
 health information. If effective, the approach would demonstrate inoculati=
on's capacity to protect belief structures that govern long-term engagement=
 with health messages.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The distinction between a discrete issue =
attitude and a broader fatalistic orientation is meaningful, and the surrou=
nding examples appropriately acknowledge prior extensions of inoculation th=
eory. However, the description moves from the breadth of the outcome constr=
uct to claims about its formation and long-term consequences. The supplied =
study context describes a two-phase experiment involving conflicting cancer=
 information; it does not establish repeated media exposure as the origin o=
f participants' beliefs or assess long-term engagement with health messages=
. Measuring a broad belief after an experimental challenge can support an e=
xtension to that outcome, but it does not by itself demonstrate protection =
against cumulative cultivation or durable effects on engagement. Calling fa=
talism a durable schema does not supply the missing longitudinal evidence.<=
/p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace these sentences with: =E2=80=9CExtending inoculation here tes=
ts whether it can protect a broader fatalistic belief orientation when part=
icipants encounter conflicting cancer information. If effective, the approa=
ch would support extending inoculation to this belief domain; protection ag=
ainst cumulative exposure and effects on long-term engagement would remain =
questions for future research.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 17</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_5" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_5">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Nonsignificant theme comparisons do not es=
tablish equivalence or robustness</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_5" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Although theme comparisons were not a focus of =
the study, using both themes added variety and strengthened the robustness =
of the inoculation test. Independent-samples t-tests revealed no significan=
t differences between themes in perceived message credibility, source credi=
bility, perceived efficacy, counterarguing, or cancer fatalism. Given this =
statistical equivalence, the two conditions were combined for analysis.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>Using two attack themes reasonably broade=
ns the stimulus set, and pooling them can be appropriate when the intended =
estimand averages over those themes. The justification given for pooling ne=
vertheless goes beyond the reported tests. A nonsignificant conventional t-=
test means that a difference was not detected; it does not establish statis=
tical equivalence, which requires a specified equivalence margin and an app=
ropriate confidence-interval or equivalence-testing procedure. Moreover, th=
ese comparisons concern theme-level averages, not whether the inoculation e=
ffects are consistent across themes: similar overall outcome means can coex=
ist with different treatment effects. Thus neither equivalence nor robustne=
ss of the inoculation effect follows from these tests alone.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace the equivalence and robustness justification with: 'No statis=
tically significant theme-level differences were detected. We pooled the th=
emes to estimate inoculation effects averaged across the two attack topics;=
 these comparisons do not establish equivalence or consistency of treatment=
 effects across topics.'</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 41</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_6" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_6">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Explicit fatalistic conclusions limit infe=
rence about resistance to conflicting information itself</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_6" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Each attack message also closed with brief fata=
listic statements. These were intended not as the central persuasive claim =
but as a simulation of natural "antigenic exposure," a feature central to t=
his study and consistent with the medical analogy underlying inoculation th=
eory</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The authors appropriately disclose that t=
he attacks contain fatalistic statements, and such statements make sense fo=
r testing resistance to fatalistic persuasion. I initially wondered whether=
 the conflicting-evidence material alone was intended to elicit fatalism, b=
ut this description shows that the attack also supplies the fatalistic inte=
rpretation explicitly. Calling those statements secondary or 'antigenic exp=
osure' does not separate their contribution from that of the conflicting ev=
idence. Consequently, this design tests resistance to a combined message co=
ntaining scientific conflict and direct fatalistic claims. It cannot establ=
ish that inoculation protects against fatalism indirectly cultivated by con=
flicting information alone=E2=80=94the distinction on which the earlier the=
oretical contribution partly rests.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>State explicitly that the experiment tests resistance to conflicting =
cancer information accompanied by explicit fatalistic interpretations, and =
limit claims about resistance to indirectly cultivated fatalism accordingly=
.</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 40</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_7" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_7">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Substantial attrition leaves Phase 2 treat=
ment comparisons vulnerable to selection bias</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_7" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>To test the comparative effects of the experime=
ntal conditions, separate one-way multivariate analyses of covariance (MANC=
OVA) were conducted for the Phase 1 and Phase 2 outcomes, using the respect=
ive analytic samples (N=3D572 in Phase 1; n=3D332 in Phase 2).</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>Using the available sample at each phase =
is understandable, and the reported degrees of freedom are consistent with =
the Phase 1 sample size. However, 240 of 572 participants=E2=80=94approxima=
tely 42%=E2=80=94did not contribute to the Phase 2 analysis. The randomizat=
ion checks do not establish that treatment groups remain comparable after t=
his loss. Even similar retention rates across conditions would not rule out=
 selection involving baseline fatalism or involvement. The passage does not=
 explain how missing follow-up data were handled beyond analyzing the small=
er sample, leaving the causal interpretation of postattack differences depe=
ndent on an unstated missingness assumption.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Add an attrition analysis reporting condition-specific retention and =
modeling Phase 2 participation as a function of condition, baseline fatalis=
m, issue involvement, and condition-by-baseline-fatalism interaction; state=
 the missingness assumption used to interpret the retained-sample compariso=
ns.</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 53</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_8" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_8">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Randomized message format does not identif=
y causal mediation</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_8" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Parallel mediation was tested with Model 4, spe=
cifying identification and transportation as simultaneous mediators and iss=
ue involvement as a covariate; indirect effects were estimated via ordinary=
 least squares (OLS) regression with 5,000 bootstrap resamples and 95% perc=
entile confidence intervals.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>Restricting this analysis to the two inoc=
ulation formats appropriately matches RQ2, and bootstrapping is a conventio=
nal way to quantify uncertainty in indirect-effect estimates. Nevertheless,=
 random assignment identifies the effect of format, not the causal effects =
of identification or transportation. These mediators were measured rather t=
han randomized, and for Phase 1 outcomes they were assessed in the same pos=
texposure measurement session. Consequently, mediator=E2=80=93outcome confo=
unding and alternative temporal orderings remain possible. Baseline fatalis=
m is also omitted here despite being included in the main outcome analyses =
and being a plausible common cause of engagement and subsequent beliefs. Bo=
otstrap confidence intervals do not resolve these identification issues, so=
 the analysis alone cannot establish that the proposed narrative mechanisms=
 caused the outcome differences.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Explicitly characterize these estimates as exploratory indirect assoc=
iations rather than identified causal mechanisms, noting that mediator=E2=
=80=93outcome confounding and temporal ordering are not resolved by randomi=
zation of message format.</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 54</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_9" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_9">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Motivational-threat wording does not estab=
lish the direction of resistance</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_9" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Motivational threat was measured with four agre=
ement-scale items (e.g., "I want to defend my current beliefs in cancer pre=
vention"), yielding  <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" j=
ax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"26" style=3D"font-size: 119=
.2%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true">=
<mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D6FC TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi>=
</mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math =
xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>=CE=B1</mi></math></mjx-as=
sistive-mml></mjx-container>  =3D .81 for conflicting cancer information an=
d  <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" tabin=
dex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"27" style=3D"font-size: 119.2%; position: rel=
ative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><mjx-mi class=3D"m=
jx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D6FC TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-as=
sistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"http://ww=
w.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>=CE=B1</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-=
container>  =3D .83 for cancer fatalism.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The distinction between apprehensive and =
motivational threat is helpful and consistent with the theoretical discussi=
on. The example item, however, measures motivation to defend an existing po=
sition without specifying that position's direction. This matters particula=
rly here because the study deliberately includes participants with high bas=
eline fatalism: defending their current beliefs could mean defending fatali=
sm rather than resisting it. The earlier discussion recognizes this traditi=
onal boundary condition, but that recognition does not resolve the measurem=
ent ambiguity in the therapeutic subgroup. High internal consistency likewi=
se cannot establish that a high score represents the same direction of defe=
nsive motivation across baseline beliefs. Unless the remaining wording expl=
icitly anchors the defended position as non-fatalistic, this measure cannot=
 uniformly be interpreted as motivation to resist fatalism.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Describe the measure as motivation to defend existing cancer-preventi=
on beliefs, and explicitly state that its direction relative to fatalism is=
 indeterminate unless the full item wording anchors the defended position a=
s non-fatalistic.</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 44</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_10" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_10">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Nonsignificant format differences do not e=
stablish equivalent threat</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_10" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Helmert contrast C2 showed no significant diffe=
rences between the two inoculation conditions on any threat measure (all <e=
m>p</em>s <em>&gt;</em> .24), indicating that narrative and non-narrative f=
ormats were equivalent in eliciting threat</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The manipulation checks appropriately est=
ablish that the combined inoculation conditions elicited greater threat tha=
n control, and C2 provides no evidence of a difference between formats. I c=
hecked whether =E2=80=9Cequivalent=E2=80=9D was supported by an equivalence=
 analysis, but the reported evidence consists of ordinary significance test=
s. Failure to reject a zero difference does not establish that any differen=
ces are sufficiently small to be practically negligible. Thus, the evidence=
 supports =E2=80=9Cno statistically detectable difference,=E2=80=9D not equ=
ivalence; potentially meaningful differences could remain compatible with t=
he estimates' uncertainty.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace =E2=80=9Cindicating that narrative and non-narrative formats =
were equivalent in eliciting threat=E2=80=9D with =E2=80=9Cproviding no sta=
tistically significant evidence that the formats differed in eliciting thre=
at.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 57</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_11" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_11">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">The postintervention contrast does not est=
ablish prophylactic maintenance</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_11" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>It also extended into the upper portion of the =
lower-fatalism range (3.49=E2=80=934.00), consistent with a prophylactic fu=
nction of preserving or reinforcing comparatively low fatalism. Because the=
 effect did not extend below 3.49, H2 received partial support at postinter=
vention.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The authors appropriately retain baseline=
 fatalism as a continuous moderator and explain how the Johnson=E2=80=93Ney=
man region intersects their theoretically motivated lower-fatalism range. T=
hat establishes a relative treatment benefit within part of that range. How=
ever, H2 specifically predicts maintenance of initially low fatalism, where=
as the reported analysis establishes only a between-condition difference in=
 baseline-to-postintervention change. Such a difference could reflect reduc=
tions in both conditions, increases in both conditions, or maintenance in o=
ne condition and change in the other. The passage does not report the condi=
tional within-condition changes needed to distinguish these possibilities. =
Moreover, this measurement precedes the Phase 2 attack, so ordinary immedia=
te persuasion among lower-fatalism participants remains an alternative to d=
emonstrated prophylactic resistance. Calling this partial support for H2 th=
erefore goes beyond the reported result.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace the quoted interpretation with: =E2=80=9CThe relative treatme=
nt benefit also extended into the upper portion of the lower-fatalism range=
 (3.49=E2=80=934.00). This establishes an immediate benefit among some init=
ially lower-fatalism participants, but does not by itself establish the mai=
ntenance or postattack protection predicted by H2.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 63</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_12" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_12">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Nonsignificant format differences do not e=
stablish noninferiority</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_12" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Although our design did not test breadth of pro=
tection across attack contexts, narrative inoculation was no less effective=
 than its non-narrative counterpart on the outcomes assessed. Narrative del=
ivery, in other words, appears not to have compromised inoculation's protec=
tive function, even if it conferred no consistent advantage.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The authors appropriately acknowledge tha=
t the design cannot establish breadth of protection, and the reported compa=
risons reasonably support saying that neither format consistently outperfor=
med the other. However, =E2=80=9Cno less effective=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cno=
t to have compromised=E2=80=9D go further: they imply that a meaningful dis=
advantage of narrative delivery has been excluded. The analytical approach =
describes conventional difference tests, not an equivalence or noninferiori=
ty analysis with a prespecified margin. A nonsignificant format contrast ca=
n remain compatible with a practically important disadvantage, particularly=
 in the smaller follow-up sample. The qualification about breadth therefore=
 does not resolve the separate uncertainty about comparative effectiveness.=
</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace these two sentences with: =E2=80=9CWe found no consistent evi=
dence of differences between narrative and non-narrative inoculation on the=
 assessed outcomes; these comparisons do not establish equivalence or rule =
out a meaningful disadvantage of either format.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 73</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_13" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_13">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">The postattack interaction is not a direct=
 test of therapeutic persistence</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_13" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Although the therapeutic evidence was clearer i=
mmediately after the intervention, the postattack interaction was nonsignif=
icant. The findings therefore support immediate belief reduction but not a =
durable therapeutic effect.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>I read this as a cautious statement about=
 insufficient evidence, rather than a claim that the therapeutic effect def=
initely disappeared. That caution is appropriate. Nevertheless, the stated =
statistical reason does not directly address the conclusion. In the specifi=
ed moderation model, the interaction tests whether the inoculation=E2=80=93=
control difference varies with baseline fatalism; it does not test whether =
inoculation still reduces fatalism among participants with high baseline fa=
talism. That conditional treatment effect could remain significant even whe=
n the interaction is not. The passage's opening also describes a postattack=
 region covering the higher-fatalism range, which makes this distinction co=
nsequential. Furthermore, a significant interaction immediately after inter=
vention and a nonsignificant interaction later do not themselves demonstrat=
e a change between phases. Thus, the nonsignificant interaction supports wi=
thholding a claim of baseline-dependent effects after attack, but is not by=
 itself grounds for withholding all evidence of a sustained therapeutic ben=
efit.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Separate the claims: state that baseline-dependent efficacy was not c=
onfirmed after attack, and base the assessment of sustained therapeutic ben=
efit on the estimated postattack inoculation=E2=80=93control contrasts and =
confidence intervals within the prespecified higher-fatalism range, rather =
than on interaction significance.</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 76</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_14" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_14">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Postattack differences do not isolate resi=
stance to the attack</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_14" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>In conclusion, this study advances inoculation =
theory by extending its scope beyond the preservation of discrete attitudes=
 to the modification of broader belief orientations and resistance to adver=
se media effects.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The evidence for modifying fatalistic bel=
iefs is reasonably grounded in the randomized intervention comparison, and =
the authors appropriately qualify the prophylactic and therapeutic interpre=
tations elsewhere. I initially read =E2=80=9Cresistance to adverse media ef=
fects=E2=80=9D as referring simply to the favorable differences observed af=
ter attack exposure. However, all participants received an attack, fatalism=
 already differed immediately after the intervention, and the postattack ch=
ange outcome was calculated relative to baseline rather than immediately be=
fore the attack. Consequently, the postattack difference could reflect pers=
istence of the intervention's initial persuasive effect without any reducti=
on in susceptibility to the attack. The design also does not independently =
establish that the attack produced adverse effects. The conclusion therefor=
e treats a possible resistance interpretation as something established by t=
he study.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace =E2=80=9Cand resistance to adverse media effects=E2=80=9D wit=
h =E2=80=9Cwith favorable intervention=E2=80=93control differences also obs=
erved after exposure to conflicting cancer information, although the design=
 cannot distinguish persistence of initial persuasion from resistance to th=
e attack.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 81</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_15" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_15">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Substantial follow-up attrition remains un=
addressed in interpreting sustained effects</span>
      <span class=3D"badge moderate">moderate</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_15" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>In the context of conflicting cancer informatio=
n, both narrative and non-narrative inoculation reduced fatalism and streng=
thened perceived efficacy, suggesting that inoculation's protective capacit=
y may rest less on message format than on the effective delivery of threat =
and refutational preemption.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The reported contrasts support favorable =
effects on fatalism and efficacy, and the limitations appropriately acknowl=
edge small effects and stimulus-specific confounds. I nevertheless checked =
whether the postattack findings had an additional qualification: only 332 o=
f the original 572 participants completed follow-up, a loss of approximatel=
y 42%. The supplied analytical account uses those completers for Phase 2 bu=
t reports neither retention by randomized condition nor an assessment of se=
lection into follow-up. Baseline balance across randomized groups does not =
establish balance among retained participants. Attrition does not automatic=
ally invalidate the results, but conditioning on completion can compromise =
the randomized comparison if completion is related to treatment and outcome=
-relevant characteristics. The broad conclusion about protection omits this=
 material uncertainty about the postattack evidence.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Add to the limitations: =E2=80=9CPhase 2 analyses included only 58.0%=
 of the randomized sample; because selection into follow-up was not evaluat=
ed here, the postattack comparisons may be affected by attrition bias and s=
hould not be assumed to retain the protection against confounding afforded =
by initial randomization.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 81</span>
  </div><div class=3D"section-label" style=3D"color:#075985">Minor Issues (=
3)</div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_1" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_1">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Protection against novel attacks does not =
establish an active counterarguing mechanism</span>
      <span class=3D"badge minor">minor</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_1" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>Crucially, inoculation's protection is not conf=
ined to the specific counterarguments contained in the message; because it =
extends an umbrella of protection to novel attacks as well (<a href=3D"http=
://localhost:8080/#page-11-0">Compton, 2025)</a>, its effects rest on a bro=
ader and more active resistance process than the mere recall of prepackaged=
 rebuttals.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The authors appropriately distinguish ino=
culation from memorizing responses to exactly matched attacks, and cross-pr=
otection is relevant to their broader application. What remains problematic=
 is the inference introduced by =E2=80=9Cbecause.=E2=80=9D Transfer to nove=
l attacks does not by itself demonstrate a more active resistance process: =
recipients might apply a remembered general principle, discount a source, o=
r recognize a familiar argumentative tactic without generating additional c=
ounterarguments. The following discussion makes active counterarguing theor=
etically plausible, but the umbrella effect alone does not establish that m=
echanism. This distinction matters because counterarguing is a central prop=
osed explanation of the study's effects.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace the causal conclusion with: =E2=80=9CThis transfer is consist=
ent with a generalized resistance process, potentially including active cou=
nterarguing, rather than protection limited to verbatim recall of attack-sp=
ecific rebuttals.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 13</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_3" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_3">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Narrative form is presented as excluding e=
xplicit claims</span>
      <span class=3D"badge minor">minor</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_3" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>As connected sequences of events featuring char=
acters and outcomes, narratives influence audiences not through explicit cl=
aims but by drawing them into story worlds and characters' perspectives.</p=
>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The intended contrast is understandable: =
transportation and identification distinguish narrative persuasion from pri=
marily expository argument. However, =E2=80=9Cnot through explicit claims=
=E2=80=9D makes these routes mutually exclusive. Narratives can contain exp=
licit claims, including claims expressed by characters or narrators, and th=
ose claims can contribute to persuasion alongside immersion. The later disc=
ussion partly corrects this impression by describing narratives that embed =
counterarguments and model refutations. Nevertheless, the opening assertion=
 remains misleading in a study whose central comparison holds inoculation c=
omponents constant while changing their presentation: narrative form does n=
ot itself imply the absence of explicit argument.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>Replace =E2=80=9Cnarratives influence audiences not through explicit =
claims but by drawing them into story worlds and characters' perspectives=
=E2=80=9D with =E2=80=9Cnarratives can influence audiences by drawing them =
into story worlds and characters' perspectives, alongside any explicit clai=
ms they contain.=E2=80=9D</p>
</div>
    <span class=3D"para-link">=C2=B6 21</span>
  </div><div class=3D"comment-card" id=3D"card-progressive_full__gpt-6.1-so=
l_4" data-id=3D"progressive_full__gpt-6.1-sol_4">
    <div class=3D"card-header">
      <span class=3D"card-title">Reported standard errors do not match the =
group summaries</span>
      <span class=3D"badge minor">minor</span>
      <button class=3D"resolve-btn" data-resolve-id=3D"progressive_full__gp=
t-6.1-sol_4" title=3D"Mark as resolved">=E2=9C=93</button>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"quote"><p>both the non-narrative inoculation (<em>n</em> =
=3D 71, <em>M</em> =3D 5.81, <em>SD</em> =3D 0.69, <em>SE</em> =3D .13, <em=
>p</em> =3D .049) and the narrative inoculation (<em>n</em> =3D 69, <em>M</=
em> =3D 5.85, <em>SD</em> =3D 0.68, <em>SE</em> =3D .13, <em>p</em> =3D .02=
4)</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"explanation"><p>The ANOVA and group summaries appear broa=
dly compatible after allowing for rounding, but the reported standard error=
s initially seem inconsistent with the sample sizes and standard deviations=
. Using SE =3D SD/=E2=88=9An gives approximately .082 for each inoculation =
group's mean, not .13. There is a plausible resolution: .13 is approximatel=
y the pooled standard error of each inoculation-versus-control mean differe=
nce. Because it is listed alongside each group's M and SD without that qual=
ification, however, readers would normally interpret it as the standard err=
or of the group mean. The issue may therefore be labeling rather than a cal=
culation error.</p>
</div>
    <div class=3D"suggested-fix"><span class=3D"sf-label">Suggested fix</sp=
an><p>If .13 is the standard error of the inoculation-versus-control contra=
st, label it explicitly as such and separate it from the descriptive group =
statistics; otherwise replace each group-mean SE with approximately .082.</=
p>
